1
00:00:02.158 --> 00:00:02.000
Next, we're going to hear from Weaviate.

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.400
Weaviate is an open source vector database.

3
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.960
They allow you to not only store information, but allow your agents to tap this information

4
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:11.960
themselves.

5
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:17.920
And some of their capabilities for searching can power amazing use cases when you need

6
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:18.920
vector search.

7
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.440
I want to make sure that we hear from them and their perspective as one of the leading

8
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.840
databases that are being adopted out there.

9
00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:26.840
So let's jump into that.

10
00:00:27.159 --> 00:00:30.159
Sebastian, thank you so much for being with us today.

11
00:00:30.159 --> 00:00:31.479
I really appreciate it.

12
00:00:31.479 --> 00:00:35.959
It's so cool that we get to have people from Weaviate joining us on this course.

13
00:00:35.959 --> 00:00:38.400
So I have a few questions for you.

14
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.919
As the agentic space continues to evolve, how important is it to have this broader ecosystem

15
00:00:44.919 --> 00:00:50.240
of tools and resources, including vector databases like Weaviate?

16
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:55.799
Yeah, I think this is absolutely critical because it's very much like you could have

17
00:00:55.799 --> 00:01:01.599
a 10x developer that knows about absolutely everything, but then you rely on this single

18
00:01:01.599 --> 00:01:02.680
point of failure.

19
00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:06.120
But also, how far can the 10x developer go?

20
00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.879
And then following the same analogies, if you have a very well-rounded team with maybe

21
00:01:10.879 --> 00:01:15.199
even sometimes overlapping skills, etc., you can achieve a lot more.

22
00:01:15.199 --> 00:01:19.919
And I think this translates really nicely into the AI ecosystem.

23
00:01:19.919 --> 00:01:22.919
We can do so much more together, right?

24
00:01:22.959 --> 00:01:27.559
And then if we wanted to do absolutely everything by ourselves, we would fail.

25
00:01:27.559 --> 00:01:32.919
And this is the best thing because each of us can focus on the thing that we do best

26
00:01:32.919 --> 00:01:36.480
while always also thinking, how can I integrate?

27
00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:37.480
How can I connect?

28
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:40.360
How can I sync with all these other tools together?

29
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.519
And how can we work as an ecosystem?

30
00:01:42.519 --> 00:01:43.599
And that's the thing.

31
00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:47.879
We can work and play as a team because we all belong together.

32
00:01:47.879 --> 00:01:51.800
We're not independent teams trying to win by ourselves.

33
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:52.800
I love that.

34
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:53.800
We all belong together.

35
00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.839
I'm going to use this one a little more often.

36
00:01:55.839 --> 00:01:56.839
It's a good one.

37
00:01:56.839 --> 00:02:02.839
So specifically on the vector space, there's so much that people have been talking about.

38
00:02:02.839 --> 00:02:05.000
Everyone has been doing RAG nowadays.

39
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.639
How do you see the role of vector knowledge evolving, especially around the idea of AI

40
00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:14.520
agents now, and they becoming more and more autonomous?

41
00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.520
The vector databases and the vector embeddings, they play a pretty critical role in all of

42
00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:20.520
this.

43
00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:26.600
If you have an agent that just relies on LLM, then you basically rely on a base of knowledge.

44
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:31.639
Well, if you work with a vector database, you kind of get access to data that is dynamic,

45
00:02:31.639 --> 00:02:34.800
that can change within a millisecond.

46
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.720
And you always get information that is up to date.

47
00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.199
The information is up to date, but also very relevant.

48
00:02:40.199 --> 00:02:42.720
So you get only the information that is relevant.

49
00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.039
The information then is a lot smaller.

50
00:02:45.119 --> 00:02:51.360
So you're not sending a 100-page PDF to your LLM, but only the relevant parts.

51
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:57.320
The other thing is that also you get information that could be relevant to a specific user.

52
00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:01.960
Maybe your agentic workflow is serving hundreds of thousands of people, each with their own

53
00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:03.440
data, right?

54
00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:06.600
You wouldn't have hundreds of thousands of LLMs per user, because that would be absolutely

55
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.039
crazy and it would take forever to update each of those.

56
00:03:10.039 --> 00:03:15.199
The other thing is you could use various fragmentations, where you could kind of separate

57
00:03:15.199 --> 00:03:18.039
into different tenants, the datasets.

58
00:03:18.039 --> 00:03:23.639
And then I would never query your part of the data and you would never query mine.

59
00:03:23.639 --> 00:03:28.759
So the databases can basically enhance the agents with more protections, more securities

60
00:03:28.759 --> 00:03:32.080
that you would expect of a mature database, right?

61
00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.520
There's the data that you want your agent to tap into.

62
00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.880
And then you want to make sure that that data is fresh data.

63
00:03:39.039 --> 00:03:43.520
There's a few options for that, but I don't think there's anything like a vector database

64
00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.440
like Weaviate has that gives you all the controls.

65
00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.759
Because it's not only that you want the most recent data, but you can control everything.

66
00:03:50.759 --> 00:03:52.520
That is definitely exciting.

67
00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:58.559
When you think about the new classes of applications, the use cases that now are possible by basically

68
00:03:58.559 --> 00:04:05.100
tightly unifying your agent orchestration with the vector retrieval, is that something

69
00:04:05.179 --> 00:04:10.380
that you hear a lot about, like specific use cases around agents with the vector retrievals

70
00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:11.979
like close to each other?

71
00:04:11.979 --> 00:04:13.979
And what would be some interesting ones?

72
00:04:13.979 --> 00:04:18.660
I'm often thinking of like, what is it that we can stop from the database to give back

73
00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:20.019
to the LLM?

74
00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:24.100
And an example being actually more than a year ago, we rewrote some of our language

75
00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:25.100
clients.

76
00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:29.420
We changed completely the APIs in Python, in TypeScript and other languages.

77
00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:33.739
And the struggle that we have today is that a lot of LLMs, they were trained with the

78
00:04:33.739 --> 00:04:35.459
old APIs.

79
00:04:35.459 --> 00:04:39.679
And then when somebody is trying to vibe code with Weaviate, they're getting the old code.

80
00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:42.820
We don't want people to use the old code because the old code wasn't great, right?

81
00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:45.739
The new one has got a great 10x.

82
00:04:45.739 --> 00:04:51.100
So by doing it through a database, we could actually go say, hey, let's delete all the

83
00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:54.859
records that talk about the old API and only use the new examples.

84
00:04:54.859 --> 00:05:00.140
So not only can we actually add more information, but we can even restrict the information from

85
00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:04.420
going like, hey, never show this part because we can delete it or filter it out.

86
00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:08.940
Because to me, agents are almost like people that are out there to help you with the most

87
00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:13.100
accurate information that is also relevant to your data, right?

88
00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:17.820
So you don't want something that is just like, hey, here's an example of like a query in

89
00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:18.820
Weaviate.

90
00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:20.859
But you're like, oh, right, you have these three collections.

91
00:05:20.859 --> 00:05:24.540
Here's how you query this specific collection with these specific properties.

92
00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:27.100
And then that's kind of like really powerful.

93
00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:31.739
Right now, it feels like people started understanding prompting and how that works and why that

94
00:05:31.739 --> 00:05:32.980
is important.

95
00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:37.179
But now, especially as people are going to agents where you have many API calls going

96
00:05:37.179 --> 00:05:42.059
with the LLMs, the complex engineering has become something that is super important.

97
00:05:42.059 --> 00:05:46.739
And we talk about that through the entire course, this idea that you need to optimize

98
00:05:46.739 --> 00:05:48.260
as much as you can.

99
00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:53.940
How should developers think about the role of a vector database in that complex engineering?

100
00:05:54.019 --> 00:05:58.260
Honestly, think of it like, again, like agents are like people to me.

101
00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:02.579
And then if you every single time send that 400 pages PDF to them and say like, hey, I

102
00:06:02.579 --> 00:06:03.980
have this question.

103
00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:08.299
And sometimes it could be something as simple as, you know, like, hey, how do I connect

104
00:06:08.299 --> 00:06:11.339
to the database, which might be accessible on the first page.

105
00:06:11.339 --> 00:06:14.660
But the thing is that the LLM will try to read the whole context, right?

106
00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:19.019
So what you end up with is just something that is super slow and not very efficient.

107
00:06:19.019 --> 00:06:21.700
And then you're going for an absolute overkill, right?

108
00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:26.100
So I think for me, it's always about like, what is it that the agent needs?

109
00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:29.459
What is that LLM needs to deliver on the task?

110
00:06:29.459 --> 00:06:30.459
That's a good point.

111
00:06:30.459 --> 00:06:35.220
Now talk about the challenges, like what are the common pitfalls or anti-patterns, for

112
00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:41.859
example, when people are trying to bring vectors and vector search into this agents, like what

113
00:06:41.859 --> 00:06:46.100
are the things that usually people do wrong and that they should watch for?

114
00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:52.140
I think one of the things like it's a classical data problem of garbage in, garbage out.

115
00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:57.140
Quite often like, yeah, embedding models are really good with unstructured data, but that

116
00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:02.420
doesn't mean that the unstructured data needs to be garbage or completely a mess, right?

117
00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:06.220
So quite often we don't think enough about this kind of stuff, right?

118
00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:10.980
How about I create my vector embeddings based on specific fields?

119
00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:15.779
Do you really need like the author's name or emails or all this other information?

120
00:07:15.779 --> 00:07:17.019
Probably not, right?

121
00:07:17.019 --> 00:07:20.100
So it's, again, like the problem of like less is more.

122
00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:26.299
The other thing that I notice quite often is, well, vector embeddings are huge, right?

123
00:07:26.299 --> 00:07:30.660
What about if you want to scale up and go into a billion objects and billion of those

124
00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:31.660
vectors, right?

125
00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:35.140
Like, I don't know if you can get that much RAM so easily or cheaply, right?

126
00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:36.619
That's going to be expensive.

127
00:07:36.619 --> 00:07:42.220
So that's kind of like one of the common pitfalls was like, well, you didn't assess the resources

128
00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:45.679
that you need for the kind of queries that you need to wrap, right?

129
00:07:45.679 --> 00:07:48.600
So there's a few things that you can do around that.

130
00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:54.079
So you could actually reduce the size of your vectors to either reduce the number of dimensions,

131
00:07:54.079 --> 00:07:59.440
or maybe you could change each dimension to something that is of size of one byte or like

132
00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:01.200
a bit even.

133
00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:05.559
And then with that, you could actually reduce the RAM needed, you know, to a quarter.

134
00:08:05.559 --> 00:08:07.279
We actually have that built in into Weaviate.

135
00:08:07.279 --> 00:08:11.920
It takes like two lines of code to enable quantization, but it's actually super powerful.

136
00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:18.440
Recently, we actually released one called rotational quantization, RQ for short.

137
00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:23.959
And we kind of thought that quantization would actually slow down the queries and etc.

138
00:08:23.959 --> 00:08:26.279
Actually the opposite is true.

139
00:08:26.279 --> 00:08:32.599
So the queries are faster and the recall when we measured versus just uncompressed vectors,

140
00:08:32.599 --> 00:08:35.520
the difference was around like 0.01%.

141
00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:39.960
So you could only notice the difference in kind of like a lab-like environment.

142
00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:42.440
We're like, why not do it by default, right?

143
00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:43.440
This is incredible.

144
00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:46.840
So these are probably like two common pitfalls.

145
00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:51.400
So like, you know, sending too much again when generating vector embeddings, because

146
00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:55.080
and then not being precise about the use case that you're trying to serve.

147
00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:58.320
And then the other one, like not thinking about like how big those vector embeddings

148
00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:01.039
could be when you run the whole thing.

149
00:09:01.039 --> 00:09:02.039
That's an interesting perspective.

